hi i'm evan carmichael and in this video  i wanted to answer a question from one  of my readers  freestyles who owns a website called  local arena  and is having a hard time figuring out  how much his business is worth  because he wants to raise equity capital  from getting an investor on board  so a little bit of background on the  business they have put around 15 000  worth of services into the business and  they're generating revenue of about 1200  per month so he's figuring out how much  is my business worth and so he's gotten  different valuations from investors and  one in my opinion  so i've been in the vc world before and  we were raising half a million fifteen  million dollars for companies  and valuation is something that we  always looked at and the cost-based  valuation  rarely works so to say well i put  fifteen thousand dollars into my  business so it's worth  x amount is really hard to justify  unless it's an asset that they can go  and sell so if you bought a piece of  land you bought some equipment that they  can sell easily has that  tangible asset value then that's where  you can look at  cost space usually you're going to look  at how much the company is going to be  worth and you want to look at the  revenue generated  now if you see the business that is not  it's not doesn't have a lot of growth  opportunity  web-based businesses will typically sell  for one to three times annual revenue  so if you see it growing slowly then you  might be  looking in that range so figure out how  much you're going to make for the year  time's up by one to three and that's  roughly what your company is going to be  worth  but if you see it as a high growth  opportunity so that 1200 you're making a  month you see that you know  being 100 times that then  you want to basically look at those  growth projections and that's typically  what a vc or venture capitalist or angel  will look at  they'll look at for the next five years  they'll want to see what your  projections are  so you know this year how much you're  gonna make next year the next year the  next year the next year so five years  out  how much you're going to be making and  what we do is usually discount that  value back so  look at how much you're going to be  earning you know for those five years  discount that back to today and you get  a rough valuation of how much you'll be  making  you have to justify that somehow and a  lot of times it's just  it's projection right so it's a guess  but you want to have confidence in those  numbers and the more confident you  are and the better you can explain your  rationale for getting to those numbers  the more faith you have from an investor  and the more likely you'll get a better  valuation  at the end of the day it's still up to  you and the investor to  come up with that valuation the further  along you are in your goals the  better valuation you get the closer you  are to startup  usually the worst value you get so it's  great that you already have some  customers you already have some revenue  coming in  a couple things to think about if you  want to get a better valuation if you  know you need to raise 50  000 what you might say is i need 10 000  at this valuation and if  i hit these certain milestones so maybe  maybe a target doesn't get five thousand  dollars a month  you say if i get the five thousand  dollars a month then i need another  twenty five thousand dollars but i have  a better valuation  then if we hit fifteen thousand dollars  a month then i need another twenty five  thousand dollars  at a better valuation or whatever it is  so you if you need 50 000  maybe you don't need it all up front and  you can phase it out over a period of  time  you feed milestones you agree with the  investor every time you raise the  capital  it's at a cheaper rate so you're not  giving up as much equity and you're  still getting the money  you need but it requires you to actually  go and hit the targets that you set out  so you better make sure you can actually  hit those targets  the last thing i would encourage you to  think about when bringing on investor  is don't just go for somebody with money  especially in this early stage if you're  looking for an angel investor  you want somebody who can help you so  somebody who has contacts who can  introduce you to people who can get you  out there and help promote your product  beyond just somebody who  gives you money and either you know  completely stays away from the business  or worse tries to interfere and doesn't  really know much about your business and  and starts to uh drag it down a little  bit so think about who the person coming  in  is it's a lot like getting married you  got to really like the person they have  to have  a lot of potential to help you so really  focus on who that person is before  making the decision  i hope that helped if you liked the  video please give it a thumbs up  and i'd also love to hear your thoughts  and feedback if you want to leave a  comment below the video  and stay tuned for the next episode  you
 

